{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01G3vvZH9ueDMuFSZS3W1vTA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SeoVi9VKGLDquVVhvFd2vc","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"Reviewer 1: Methods + Experiments","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 in a full-mode dream review. Your scope: **methods + experiments only**. Do NOT evaluate novelty or related work.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help VLA Action Correction?\" — A diagnostic study testing whether multivariate conformal prediction (CP) features enable dimension-selective action correction in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. Central finding is negative: at K=10 samples and d=7 DOF, Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage intensity reaches 0.71, collapsing covariance toward identity and rendering per-DOF conformal features uninformative. System is called ACR-VLA, tested on LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n\nThis is the **worst** tier dream (maximally pessimistic narrative). Conflicts between narrative and evidence must be flagged.\n\n## Paper Details (from full PDF)\n\n### Method (Section 2):\n- Problem: flow-matching VLA π_θ generates action chunks a_t ∈ R^{H×d}, H=10, d=7. K=10 denoising samples.\n- CGS (Conformal Geometry Statistics): 45D feature vector from K samples — per-DOF means/stds (14), Ledoit-Wolf covariance entries (22), conformal radii (7), volume/condition number (2).\n- CRM (Conformal Residual Module): ~131K param MLP: Δa_t = CRM(φ(o_t), ā_t, f_t). Corrected action: ã_t = ā_t + Δa_t.\n- COC (Chunk Overlap Consistency): temporal overlap between consecutive chunks used as per-step coverage proxy.\n- ACI: α̂_{t+1} = α̂_t + γ(COC_t − (1−δ)).\n\n### Experimental Setup (Section 3):\n- Base VLA: SmolVLA, K=10, H=10.\n- Benchmarks: LIBERO (nominal, 10 tasks), LIBERO-Plus (L3–L5, 7 perturbation categories).\n- Training: CRM trained on LIBERO demos with synthetic perturbations (camera noise, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset).\n- Ablations: A1 (no ACI), A2 (no CRM), A3 (no conformal features), A4 (scalar uncertainty), A5 (Bonferroni vs Mahalanobis), A6 (episode vs COC ACI), A7 (DiU vs CGS).\n\n### Results:\n- Table 1: ACR-VLA full: 65.1% LIBERO-Plus (+3.1pp), 93.7% nominal (−2.8pp).\n- Table 2: A3 (no conformal) = 64.3%, full = 65.1%, gap = +0.8pp (below ~1.5pp noise). A4 (scalar) = 65.3%, slightly better than full 45D.\n- Gradient analysis: observation features get 5.25× higher gradient magnitude than conformal features.\n- Section 4.3: Shrinkage = 0.71, off-diagonal avg 0.06, condition number 14.2, radius ratio 1.08 (near-sphere). CGS vs Bonferroni gap = 0.1pp.\n- Table 3: COC-task success Spearman ρ = 0.19 (threshold ≥0.3, FAIL), volume narrowing 6.2% (threshold ≥10%, FAIL).\n- A1: removing ACI → −0.4pp. A6: episode-level = COC (65.0 vs 65.1).\n- A2: no CRM → 62.9%, confirming CRM is the active ingredient.\n- Table 4: Per-perturbation gains — augmentable avg +4.17pp, non-augmentable avg +0.53pp. Gains track training augmentation coverage, not uncertainty.\n- Table 5: DiU 7D = 64.9%, CGS 45D = 65.1%, Combined = 65.4%. All within 1.1pp of A3 baseline.\n- SnapFlow: 63.8% (−1.3pp), 3.17× faster, 42% less diversity.\n- SRPO stacking: 82.1 → 83.4% (+1.3pp, below 2pp significance).\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method description completeness**: Are claims supported by experiments? Are key hyperparameters and training procedures clear?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does it cover key baselines, ablations, cross-dataset, and scale dimensions?\n3. **Data presentation**: Can a reviewer reproduce conclusions from the tables/figures? Are error bars and statistical significance adequate?\n\nFlag any conflicts with the \"worst\" tier narrative direction (maximally pessimistic). If the paper is more pessimistic than data warrants, or insufficiently pessimistic given the data, note it.\n\nProvide your review in structured format with: (a) summary of strengths, (b) summary of weaknesses, (c) detailed issues with severity (major/minor), (d) your preliminary score (strong reject / borderline reject / borderline accept / weak accept / accept).\n\nRespond concisely — no pleasantries."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3046,"cache_read_input_tokens":41563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3046},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"908704ee-979c-4338-9cc9-65d0350375b6","uuid":"7c5813db-bd6d-4ece-ae4f-2e31f46a17c9"}
